Performed Grid-Search over the 8 different EfficientNet versions (three runs each, ImageNet init) on the cropped frames.
-> As long as not B0, it does not really matter; B4 should be a save choice
-> Details of this can be found in the runs folder; no model folders in here anymore (not needed, B4 is the reference)

Compared B4 with ImageNet weights against B4 with weigths further trained on Kaggle dataset.
-> Clear advantage of adding the Kaggle pre-training

Further compared to DINOv3 ViT-L/16 with pre-trained weights given LVD-1689M.
Tested four versions: Either frozen DINOv3 or not (only train classifier head); Either use cls token or mean of patch tokens.
-> Frozen worse than fine-tuned
-> Frozen patchmean worse than cls
-> Fine-tuned versions very similar (expected because models can be tuned to be identical -> Info in patchmean and cls probably the same in the end)

Additionally trained fine-tuned cls DINOv3 version with same pre-processing as the EfficientNet (DINOv3 augs quite conservative).
-> Seems to be a bit worse than the other two fine-tuned; might be because the val set is close to val set, so the robustness that might be
created by using the rotations and flips and so on is not needed for good results on the val set and therefore performance is slightly worse
    -> However, in the end the performance for the MIL model is the most important and not the one of the classifier in isolation

misc contains vis of some train and val images and the GradCam result from some model (just for vis of idea of GC).

models now contains the 5 runs for the 5 splits of DINOv3 without Kaggle fine-tuning, with the complex augmentations,
un-frozen backbone and trained with the cls token

train_classifier.py:
(not modified for the new 5-split setting and the final classifier is also simpler than the DINO one,
needs to be changed before next use)
train B4 classifier on the cropped frames

utils.py:
some utils used for training both the B4 and DINO classifier
